#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096353 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "7b49d6ec-658f-5d2a-b043-a0646ad5f904" :question/math-academy-id "e-4046" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7 x^2 - 2x - 13}{(x^2+1)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{9x+B}{x^2 +1} - \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$,\n\nwhat is the value of $B$?" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x^2+1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x^{2} - 2x - 13 &= (9x + B)(x + 1) - 2(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7(0)^{2} - 2(0) - 13 &= (9 \\cdot (0) + B)(0 + 1) - 2((0)^{2} + 1) \\\\\n- 13 &= B - 2 \\\\\nB &= -11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "f2483ab1-2728-5a0f-b3e2-7388b5051493" :question/math-academy-id "e-504" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1)) &= \\frac{Ax + B}{x^{2} + 4} + \\frac{C}{x - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $x^2+4$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $(x^2+4)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= (Ax + B)(x - 1) + C(x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(1) + 3 &= (A(1) + B)(1 - 1) + C(1^{2} + 4) \\\\\n5 &= 5C \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(0) + 3 &= (A(0) + B)(0 - 1) + C(0^{2} + 4) \\\\\n3 &= -B + 4C \\\\\n3 &= -B + 4(1) \\\\\nB &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A$, we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(2) + 3 &= (A(2) + B)(2 - 1) + C(2^{2} + 4) \\\\\n7 &= 2A + B + 8C \\\\\n7 &= 2A + 1 + 8(1) \\\\\n2A &= -2 \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1$, $B=1$, and $C=1$. Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{1-x}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad8c427-9061-5d56-96b7-aaf7ea7df328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{1}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266451} {:answer/id #uuid "b43fd6df-ac80-58f7-b04d-6a75e53f78af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-5}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} {:answer/id #uuid "9a20ffb8-6d84-58b9-aaad-4aa88f241da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{4}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266453} {:answer/id #uuid "4242797a-4904-585e-80e9-32b186ba1f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266454} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdbff02-3dae-518a-b942-37e882a23c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{5}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4242797a-4904-585e-80e9-32b186ba1f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266454} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2d2ec3-c0fa-51a0-b5be-8d3b2b96ece2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10213766-0256-5950-8424-1b014b86c8e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-102561" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+6$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)$ and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2x-3=(Ax+B)(x+5)+C({x}^{2}+6).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-5)^{2}+2(-5)-3 & =(A(-5)+B)(-5+5)+C((-5)^{2}+6) \\\\ 75-10-3 & =31C \\\\ C & =\\frac{62}{31} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3 & =(A(0)+B)(0+5)+C((0)^{2}+6) \\\\ -3 & =5B+6C \\\\ -3 & =5B+6(2) \\\\ 5B & =-3-12 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-15}{5} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A$ we choose $x=1$, and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}+2(1)-3 & =(A(1)+B)(1+5)+C((1)^{2}+6) \\\\ 2 & =6A+6B+7C \\\\ 2 & =6A+6(-3)+7(2) \\\\ 6A & =2+18-14 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1,B=-3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}=\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246a9b61-0e8a-5a21-b8d8-06264175159d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x-3}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266479} {:answer/id #uuid "4f394579-1cbf-5a79-aef6-28430d55ca21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-8}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{5}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266480} {:answer/id #uuid "dab86164-5e86-582f-a790-f4f2d111cc7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{6}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266481} {:answer/id #uuid "ffed33a2-8876-5902-a220-7a3b0930283a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)}-\\frac{3}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266482} {:answer/id #uuid "a41682cd-77eb-5199-9a83-a429790912b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+2}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246a9b61-0e8a-5a21-b8d8-06264175159d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x-3}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266479} :answer-field/id #uuid "8615af00-5ca7-54d3-a212-9aa564da9141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ba96632-d869-56e1-a1ef-bc4e5fea1ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-11015" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-28x+17}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-28x+17}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+1$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-28x+17=(Ax+B)(x-5)+C({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(5)^{2}-28(5)+17 & =(A(5)+B)(5-5)+C({5}^{2}+1) \\\\ 175-140+17 & =26C \\\\ 52 & =26C \\\\ C & =\\frac{52}{26} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-28(0)+17 & =(A(0)+B)(0-5)+C({0}^{2}+1) \\\\ 17 & =-5B+C \\\\ 17 & =-5B+2 \\\\ 5B & =2-17 \\\\ B & =-\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}-28(1)+17 & =(A(1)+B)(1-5)+C({1}^{2}+1) \\\\ -4 & =-4A-4B+2C \\\\ -4 & =-4A-4(-3)+2(2) \\\\ 4A & =12+4+4 \\\\ A & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=5,$ $B=-3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-28x+17}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)}=\\frac{5x-3}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3690fc1f-4c84-57fa-b606-75788d3e9b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3690fc1f-4c84-57fa-b606-75788d3e9b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266486} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ca4568-bc12-5c7f-af12-b55a44d28fac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416ace5f-a095-5b1d-9f53-7a89a7595257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "416ace5f-a095-5b1d-9f53-7a89a7595257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266488} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b651625-8eef-5707-9a2f-83184ef2d966" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266487} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f03868e-b94d-5f9b-a926-e9c2b6a05ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f03868e-b94d-5f9b-a926-e9c2b6a05ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266490} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e237352-c2c2-53e3-b6f8-254875dcd5e5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3fb4ddf-fa1a-5f35-80c1-327fb7940b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-346957" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $8({x}^{2}+4)$ |  | $8(x+2)$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{C}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n5=(Ax+B)(x+2)+C({x}^{2}+4).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =C(4+4) \\\\ 5 & =8C \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(0)+B)(0+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((0)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =2B+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2B & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(1)+\\frac{5}{4})(1+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((1)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =3A+\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\ 3A & =-\\frac{15}{8} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{8}x+\\frac{5}{4})}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{(\\frac{5}{8})}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\,x+10}{8({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{5}{8(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
